+# Content — review questions
+
+The content skill's rules, worded as questions a reviewer answers yes, no or n/a from the screenshots or the page's source; the `review` skill asks them.
+
+## Words
+
+1. Is each thing shown before it is told: files as a tree, an object as its live instance, a task as its checklist, a layout as a screenshot? [content: let the layout say it]
+2. Does the layout itself show the structure (hierarchy, proximity, grouping), with no sentence spent explaining it? [content: let the layout say it]
+3. Is every paragraph 60 words or fewer? [owner 2026-09-30]
+4. Could the owner get the page's point in ten seconds? [content: the length check]
+5. Does every title say exactly what its thing is? [content: words, when they earn it]
+6. Does every picture carry at most a one-line caption? [content: words, when they earn it]
+7. Is each concept named once, the same way every time? [content: name each concept once; CLAUDE.md: the same name every time]
+8. Does every mention of a concept with its own page link to that page, and every named file or module link to its page on the site? [content: connect the dots; CLAUDE.md: presentation]
+9. Where there is a file of words (a readme, a doc, a report), does a picture or a live widget sit beside it? [owner 2026-09-30] [CLAUDE.md: show it with the widget, then use words]
+10. Does the most important thing lead, usually the list of what this is made of, with detail one click down? [content: the tip of the iceberg comes first]
+11. Is the text written in full, plain sentences a new coder can follow, never clipped fragments or undefined jargon? [CLAUDE.md: law 2, clear beats brief]
